 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to procurement lobbying; and to amend chapter 1 of the laws of 2005 amending the state finance law and other laws relating to restricting contacts in the procurement process and the recording of contacts relating thereto, in relation to extending the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6924",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6924-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1-c, Leg L; amd §§139-j & 139-k, St Fin L; amd §16, Chap 1 of 2005",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6924\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the state finance law, in relation to procurement\nlobbying; and to amend chapter 1 of the laws of 2005 amending the\nstate finance law and other laws relating to restricting contacts in\nthe procurement process and the recording of contacts relating\nthereto, in relation to extending the effectiveness of certain\nprovisions thereof\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nThis bill makes improvements to and clarifies the extent of the\nprocurement lobbying law, and extends the expiration date of that law\nfrom March 10, 2010 to July 31, 2014.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 of the bill outlines the legislative intent of the initial\nprocurement lobbying statute, including its legislative history.\n\nSection 2 amends State Finance Law (SFL) Section 139-j to add\ntechnical and substantive improvements, including a clearer definition\nof when the restricted contact period begins and the responsibilities\nof governmental agencies during that period, as well as what\ncommunications are not subject to the restricted contact provisions of\nthe law.\n\nSection 3 amends SFL Section 139-k to conform its provisions to the\namendments made to SFL Section 139-j in Section 2 of this bill.\n\nSection 4 amends section 16 of Chapter 1 of the Laws of 2005 to extend\nthe expiration date of SFL Sections 139-j.and 139-k to July 31, 2014.\n\nSection 5 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nSFL §§ 139-j and 139-k set forth restrictions on contacts during the\nprocurement process and.provide for recording of such contacts. These\nstatutes were originally subject to a December 31, 2007 expiration\ndate, but were extended to July 31, 2009 by Chapter 501 of the laws of\n2007 and to March 10, 2010 by Chapter 169 of 2009. These two statutory\nprovisions are set to expire on March 10, 2010.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nThis is a new proposal.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\nConcerns about ensuring that the public procurement process is\ncompetitive, open and transparent, with a level playing field for\nobtaining contract awards by participating vendors, resulted in the\nenactment in 2005 of the procurement lobbying law set forth in SFL .\n239-j and 139-k. These two provisions restrict contacts during the\nprocurement process and provide for the recording of such contacts.\n\nWithout an extender, these requirements would be eliminated. Such\nprovisions are important to maintain the integrity of the public\nprocurement infrastructure and to continue with best practices in the\nestablishment and administration of State contracts.\n\nSince its enactment in 2005, these provisions have greatly increased\npublic and vendor confidence in the fairness and transparency of the\nState procurement process. However, concerns have been expressed that\nthey have also resulted in the unintended consequences of creating a\nchilling effect on legitimate vendor-agency communications during\ngovernment procurements.\n\nSuch legitimate communications, as long as they are properly recorded\nand evenhandedly received, are an important source of feedback for\nagencies during complex procurements. The new provisions of this bill,\nin conjunction with its extension until 2014, would help to create\nclarity and certainty for both vendors and governmental agencies as to\ntheir rights and responsibilities during the procurement process.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\nMinimal.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
